$\gamma_k\!\sim\!\mathcal U(0.35,1.10)$,
$\psi_k\!\sim\!\mathcal U(-\pi,\pi]$\\
Trials & $480$ per accuracy cell; $960$ per benchmark cell\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:params} gives the matched scene, whose
$\max_k d_{{\rm UR},k}=4.2~{\rm m}<R_{\rm F}=20.5~{\rm m}
<\min_k d_{{\rm RB},k}=28.6$~m satisfies (A3). Writing $\bmu=\bA(\bchi)\bx$ for
the noiseless observation and $\bmu_{\rm act}$ for its restriction to the
$M_{\rm act}$ entries carried by the receive channels that
$\bS_{\rm mode}$ retains, the operating point is set by
\begin{equation}
	{\rm SNR}=10\log_{10}\frac{\|\bmu_{\rm act}\|_2^2}
	{M_{\rm act}\sigma^2},
	\label{eq:snrdef}
\end{equation}
a per-sample pre-noise ratio with absolute path loss absorbed into the
normalization.

With $e_{p,r}=\|\hat\bp_r-\bp^\star\|_2$ and
$e_{t,r}=|\widehat{\Delta t}_r-\Delta t^\star|$ over $R$ trials, and
${\rm fail}_r$ the event that a route terminates without returning an
estimate,
\begin{align}
	P_{\rm cat}^{(p)}&=\frac1R\sum_{r=1}^{R}
	\mathbf 1\{{\rm fail}_r\ \lor\ e_{p,r}>0.1~{\rm m}\},\nonumber\\
	P_{\rm cat}^{(t)}&=\frac1R\sum_{r=1}^{R}
	\mathbf 1\{{\rm fail}_r\ \lor\ e_{t,r}>1~{\rm ns}\},
	\label{eq:pcat}
\end{align}
Position and clock conditional RMSEs are each taken over the complement of
their own event in \eqref{eq:pcat}; the Phase-II control of
Table~\ref{tab:c3_control} instead reports both on a single per-route
non-catastrophic set, as its note states. Phase~I acquires the basin on trial $r$ when $\hat\bp_{{\rm I},r}$ is finite
with $\|\hat\bp_{{\rm I},r}-\bp^\star\|_2\le0.1$~m. The declared operating
region is ${\rm SNR}\ge-10$~dB, with results at $-15$ or $-20$~dB labeled as
below it. Every cell generates and estimates on the same center-frequency
spatial manifold, so $\epsilon_{\rm sq}$ of Section~\ref{subsec:defchain}
contributes no residual. A supplementary per-subcarrier diagnostic at nine
pre-specified samples produces instead a deterministic offset of at most
$0.080$~mm in position, $0.18$ of that sample's PEB, but up to $5.4$~ps in
clock, exceeding that sample's CEB at four samples.

Methods within a cell share the scene, RIS codes, Jones states, gains and
noise.

\subsection{End-to-End Benchmark}
\label{subsec:res_benchmark}
\begin{figure*}[!tb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_benchmark_main.pdf}
    \caption{Native-readout end-to-end benchmark on the matched
    center-frequency spatial manifold, $960$ shared-realization trials per
    SNR. Medians include catastrophic trials. The free-Jones PEB and CEB
    ($20$-draw median $3.38$~ps at $-10$~dB, $\sigma$-scaled) are
    local-information references, not bounds on median error; vertical dashed
    lines mark the thresholds in~\eqref{eq:pcat}.}
    \label{fig:benchmark_combined}
\end{figure*}

Fig.~\ref{fig:benchmark_combined} compares five end-to-end routes on shared
realizations. The closest in-model control, denoted baseline-initialized
4D-JVP, pairs the same exact forward model with a frozen baseline Phase-I
initializer and an explicit range-scaled four-dimensional Jones variable
projection, whereas the proposed route pairs MKSC-GI acquisition with
CCOP-JVP; the figure therefore compares complete
pipelines, not isolating C3. Their $-10$~dB median position errors differ by only $0.006$~mm.
They differ in acquisition reliability: at $-10$~dB the proposed route lowers
the position and clock catastrophic rates by $6.0\times$ and $6.7\times$
(supplementary).

Each external adaptation saturates at a different stage. Algebraic canonical
polyadic decomposition (CPD)~\cite{lin2021tensor,sidiropoulos2017tensor}
improves in median error across the range while its catastrophic rate floors
near $30\%$, so the map from converged factors to position is the limit
rather than the resolution; variational Bayesian inference with sparse
Bayesian learning (VBI/SBL)~\cite{tipping2001sbl,li2024vbi} lowers its
catastrophic rate with SNR yet holds a $26.1$-mm median throughout, a
native-fusion floor; and near-field orthogonal matching pursuit with
SAGE~\cite{pati1993omp,fleury1999sage,yan2025ris}, as adapted to the shared
random training used here, is flat in both, its far-field direction
acquisition never delivering a usable initializer. At $-10$~dB the proposed
route lowers both position and clock catastrophic rates by more than
twentyfold against the best external adaptation, and McNemar's exact test
rejects equality of the paired catastrophic indicator against each of the
four ($p\le1.91\times10^{-6}$). Both statements read the adaptations at their
native output stage and characterize those adaptations, not the cited
methods; the OMP comparison in particular uses the reference's regular-RIS
route, its sub-RIS variant requiring orthogonal phase
profiles~\cite{yan2025ris} that the shared random training cannot supply.

A separately seeded control transplants the exact-model position--clock
refinement onto each front end.
Against the baseline-initialized route the paired catastrophic difference
remains significant at $-10$~dB (McNemar exact, $p=0.0129$), and algebraic
CPD still keeps $42.92\%$ catastrophic events under the same polish: for
these two routes the separation is in acquisition, not refinement. Adaptation
details are supplementary.

\subsection{C2: Sufficient Compression and Acquisition}
\label{subsec:res_c2}
Theorem~\ref{thm:compress} establishes that the projection preserves the
profiled maximizer and the free-Jones information, so what the experiment
asks is what an exactly sufficient projection does to the finite-sample
ESPRIT front end. The identity permits discarding $90$ of $96$ receive
dimensions before a subspace method sees the remainder, and at $-10$~dB the
resulting leakage ratio $\sigma_{K+1}/\sigma_K$ falls from $0.824$ to
$0.357$, carrying the catastrophic position rate from $2.50\%$ to none of the
$480$ trials; from $0$~dB the two routes coincide, the finite-sample
advantage having saturated. Exact-model accuracy is unaltered.

Table~\ref{tab:c2_ladder} places the nested mechanism ladder at both the
operating-region boundary and a lower-SNR diagnostic point. At $-10$~dB,
compression lowers $P_{\rm cat}$ from $2.92\%$ to $0/480$ and geometry
raises basin capture to $100\%$. This ladder is independently seeded
from the preceding single-factor test. At $-20$~dB, compression removes
about $70\%$ of catastrophic events, while geometry raises basin capture
from $76.88\%$ to $93.96\%$. Four starts add a smaller gain.
Anchor refresh changes neither event count at this point, but lowers
p95 reconstruction NMSE from $0.0953$ to $0.00112$.
Growing the panel
from $16^2$ to $64^2$ elements lowers conditional RMSE from $2.02$ to
$0.43$~mm while holding the catastrophic rate at or below $1.04\%$, so the
estimator realizes the aperture gain that motivates near-field operation.
Collecting it is again an acquisition property: the proposed Phase~I holds a
basin rate above $98.9\%$ across that sixteenfold change in element count,
whereas the baseline rises only from $14.79\%$ to $95.83\%$.

